/// Interquartile range.
///
/// The distance between the third and the first quartile, `quantile(var, 0.75) - quantile(var,
/// 0.25)`, under the quantile definition `type:` names (the nine definitions of Hyndman and Fan,
/// as `quantile` offers them; 7 unless given, R's default). A robust measure of spread: half of
/// the values lie within it, and one outlier cannot move it. The quartiles are taken over the
/// values present, with the reducers' warning for the rows with `NA` skipped (section 4.1 of
/// the Language Reference); one value gives 0.
///
/// data: The frame.
/// var: The variable.
/// type: The quantile definition, 1 to 9; 7 unless given.
/// returns: The range, a Float, printed as `mean` prints.
/// details: The two quartiles come from `quantile` under one definition and are subtracted; nothing else is computed, so the result agrees with `quantile` to the bit.
/// reference: Hyndman, R. J. and Fan, Y. (1996) Sample quantiles in statistical packages, The American Statistician 50(4), 361–365.
/// reference: R Core Team, the `IQR` function of the stats package.
/// missing values: The two quartiles are taken over the values present, with a warning for the rows skipped. A variable with no value left gives `NA`.
/// coming from: d.iqr(wage) | IQR(d$wage, na.rm = TRUE) | summarize wage, detail, then display r(p75) - r(p25)
/// coming from: d.iqr(wage, type: 6) | IQR(d$wage, na.rm = TRUE, type = 6) | no option of its own
/// coming from: The definitions are numbered as R numbers them. Stata's `summarize, detail` takes its percentiles by another of the nine than R's default, so its figure may differ a little from R's and from this one.
/// related: For the two quartiles themselves, use `quantile`.
/// related: For another spread that extreme values do not move, use `mad`.
/// related: For the standard deviation, use `sd`.
/// see also: quantile, mad, sd, median
/// example: The distance between the quartiles of the wages: half of the wages lie within it. The warning counts the rows with no wage.
/// > let wages = dataset(wages)
/// > wages.iqr(wage)
/// 16.17
/// warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 2)
/// example: Under another definition of the quantile.
/// > wages.iqr(wage, type: 6)
/// 16.175
/// warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 3)
/// example: It is the difference of the two quartiles.
/// > wages.quantile(wage, p: 0.75) - wages.quantile(wage, p: 0.25)
/// 16.17
/// warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 4)
/// warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 4)
fn iqr(data: Frame, var: Var, `type`: Int = 7) -> Float {
    data.quantile(var, 0.75, type: `type`) - data.quantile(var, 0.25, type: `type`)
}
